// An ABANDONED `FrameStream` (http-streaming arc): opened, partially read, and then simply
// dropped — no `close()`, no drain. The program's own output is trivial on purpose; the real
// assertion is delivered by the **leak oracle**, which runs this same file and proves residency
// returns to 0 on both backends (`--check-leaks`, and the `KNOWN_LEAKS`-must-be-empty gate).
//
// What is being proven, and what is not:
//
//   - The language value and its extern box are reclaimed when the last reference dies. That is the
//     part the leak oracle sees, and the part that could regress silently.
//
//   - The host-side stream entry is released at host teardown. There is deliberately **no**
//     host-coupled finalizer in this runtime (see `docs/Native-Extensions.md`): values die in
//     release paths that carry no host, so an extern box cannot reach the `Network` capability from
//     its `Drop`. That is exactly why `close()` exists on the surface, and why a long-running
//     server should call it rather than leaning on teardown — a stream abandoned inside a serve
//     loop holds its connection until the process ends.
//
// Three streams are abandoned in three different states — never read, partially read, and fully
// drained — because each releases a different amount of host-side state.
// expect: stdout "opened three, read one frame from one of them"
// expect: exit 0
use std.http.client
use std.http.Framing
use std.http.HttpError

// `Result<void, HttpError>`, not `void`: each `?` below early-returns a failed *open*, so the
// signature has to be able to carry one (E0012 otherwise). The top-level `?` propagates it out of the
// program, where an unhandled `Err` aborts with its `message()` and a non-zero exit — so a run that
// never opened a stream can never be mistaken for one that opened three and abandoned them.
async fn run(): Result<void, HttpError> {
    api = client.new("https://svc.test")

    // Never read at all.
    _untouched = client.stream(api.prepare("get", "/stream/sse"), Framing.Sse)?

    // Partially read, then abandoned mid-body — the case that actually holds a live reader.
    partial = client.stream(api.prepare("get", "/stream/sse"), Framing.Sse)?
    _first = partial.recv().await

    // Fully drained but never closed: nothing left to read, entry still registered.
    drained = client.stream(api.prepare("get", "/stream/empty"), Framing.Sse)?
    _end = drained.recv().await

    echo "opened three, read one frame from one of them"
    return Ok()
}
run().await?
